.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_CREATE 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_create \- create a ccol_event_loop with default memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_event_loop_create(max_events_per_wait, num_lock_stripes,
                  num_reactor_threads, err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The same as ccol_event_loop_create_mp() with mmgmt_procs = NULL,
which selects the standard malloc and free.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_events_per_wait  Size of the epoll_wait batch buffer of the
                      poller thread, from 1 to INT_MAX inclusive, and
                      small enough that max_events_per_wait *
                      sizeof(struct epoll_event) fits in a size_t. It
                      limits how many ready events one epoll_wait call
                      drains, not how many registrations the loop can
                      hold.
num_lock_stripes     Number of independent lock stripes for the
                      registry of fds and entries, at least 1. With 1
                      there is a single shared lock. A larger value
                      lowers the contention between
                      ccol_event_loop_add, ccol_event_loop_remove and
                      ccol_event_loop_modify, which grows when threads
                      work on many different fds and registrations at
                      the same time.
num_reactor_threads  Total number of OS threads the loop uses for
                      polling and dispatching, at least 1. Whatever
                      the value, exactly one dedicated thread calls
                      epoll_wait on the shared epoll instance. With 1
                      that thread also dispatches every callback
                      inline; a larger value adds
                      (num_reactor_threads - 1) separate dispatch
                      worker threads.
err_str               Optional pointer that gets an error string on a
                      failure. Pass NULL to ignore it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new ccol_event_loop handle on success, or CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID
when an argument is invalid, an allocation failed, epoll_create1,
eventfd or pthread_create failed, the process had no thread-specific
key left for the dispatch of a loop, the library's process-exit
destructors have run and the last loop is gone, or no handle slot
could be allocated. ccol_event_loop_create_mp(3) has the details.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All of the loop's background reactor threads are already running when
the call returns. When you are done with the loop, destroy it with
ccol_event_loop_destroy().
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_create_mp (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_add (3)
